  "answer_text": "## What the verifier is telling you\n\nThe decisive line is:\n\n```\n4: (85) call bpf_cpumask_populate#61745\nR1 type=scalar expected=fp\n```\n\n`R1` holds the first argument of `bpf_cpumask_populate()` \u2014 the destination\ncpumask. In the program that register was loaded with a constant:\n\n```\n2: (b7) r1 = 1193046     ; 0x123456\n```\n\ni.e. it is a **scalar**, not a pointer. The verifier needs that argument to be a\nreal pointer (the `expected=fp,...` list is the set of pointer types it will\naccept \u2014 stack frame pointer, packet, map value, etc.). A bare integer cast to a\npointer (`(struct bpf_cpumask *)0x123456`) is rejected before the call is ever\nallowed.\n\nSo this isn\u2019t a subtle memory-size or bounds issue with `&bits`/`sizeof(bits)`\n(that argument, `R2=fp-8`, `R3=8`, passed fine). The failure is simply that the\ndestination cpumask is a fabricated/invalid scalar pointer.\n\n## Why it fails\n\n`bpf_cpumask_populate(struct cpumask *cpumask, void *src, size_t src__sz)`\nrequires `cpumask` to be a verifiably valid pointer. `0x123456` carries no type\nand no provenance, so the verifier marks it `scalar` and refuses the helper call.\n\n## Smallest fix to make it verify\n\nGive the first argument a genuine, verifier-tracked cpumask pointer instead of a\nhand-made address. Allocate one with `bpf_cpumask_create()` (and release it):\n\n```c\nSEC(\"tp_btf/task_newtask\")\nint BPF_PROG(entry, struct task_struct *task, u64 flags)\n{\n\tstruct bpf_cpumask *mask;\n\tu64 bits;\n\tint ret;\n\n\tmask = bpf_cpumask_create();\n\tif (!mask)\n\t\treturn 1;\n\n\tret = bpf_cpumask_populate((struct cpumask *)mask, &bits, sizeof(bits));\n\n\tbpf_cpumask_release(mask);\n\n\tif (!ret)\n\t\treturn 2;\n\treturn 0;\n}\n```\n\nNow `R1` is a `PTR_TO_BTF_ID` (a trusted, allocated `bpf_cpumask`) rather than a\nscalar, which satisfies the argument-type check and the program passes\nverification.\n\n## Note\n\nIf this was actually intended as a *negative* selftest (the variable is literally\nnamed `invalid`, and `0x123456` is the classic \"bogus pointer\" sentinel), then\nnothing is wrong with the program at all \u2014 the log shown *is* the expected\nrejection. In that case the \u201crepair\u201d is not in the BPF C code but in the test\nharness: tag the program with the matching expectation, e.g.\n\n```c\n__failure __msg(\"R1 type=scalar expected=fp\")\n```\n\nso the framework treats this exact verifier message as the success criterion.\n\nWhich of the two you want depends on whether the test is meant to be a success\ncase (use the `bpf_cpumask_create()` patch) or a failure case (add the\n`__failure`/`__msg` annotations matching the log).",
